Triumph of right-wing pulsators in Austria, they have a total of 29%
According to the preliminary results, the early parliamentary elections in Austria brought about a significant weakening of the two parties forming the current grand coalition, as well as a notorious strengthening of the populist parties.
The Austrian People's Party, where the position of the current President Wilhelm Molterer was seriously threatened, suffered particularly significant losses.
On the other hand, the electoral leader of the Union for the Future of Austria, the governor of Carinthia Joerg Haider, is preparing a triumphant return to the political scene at the national level.
According to the preliminary results, the Socialists remain, with 29.0 per per cent of the vote, the country’s largest political force, however, compared to the 2006 elections they lost 5.5 per cent of the vote.
With 25.6 per per cent of the vote, the zVP losing almost nine per cent of the vote, has finished even worse.
These were the worst results of the two major parties in the post-war period, especially for the People's Party, which triggered the early elections, the result was literally catastrophic.
Still in early July, when zVP departed the coalition, the People's Party had a considerable lead over the SP.
Voses are visibly chastising them for letting the government collapse.
From the earliest reactions to the results, speculation about the possible resignation of party leader and current Vice-Chancellor Wilhelm Molterer was uttered.
Observers believe that this could happen as soon as on Monday, at the party’s extraordinary leadership council.
Such an evolution a priori would make it possible to move towards the outskirts of the outcome of the election, which remains the most likely, i.e. the reappointment of the Grand Coalition between the SP and 'VP.
Nevertheless, in view of the strengthening of the two right-wing populist parties - the free parties would have obtained 18 per per cent and 11 per per cent of the vote - at the same time the socialists expressed the fear of a rehearsal of 1999, when the People's Party agreed with the Populated Populates (at the time, at the time, the FPO was still unified, it was in 2005 that the B .
Haider, who has already stated that he is ready to collaborate with any party and that he is planning to return to Vienna on the national political scene, apparently focuses on these developments.
The last party to have access to the parliament was the Greens, but they lost a slight loss among the Austrian political parties from the third in fifth place.
It was clear that no other party crossed the four-per-per-per-centimeter limit and did not accede to the parliament.
