The German special forces were on the ground for the release of the hostages
The Egyptian Government had requested Germany's assistance in resolving the hostage crisis - the experts came.
When the situation became serious, the special units stood ready, but they never took action.
More and more details on the circumstances of the release of eleven European tourists kidnapped in Egypt are being revealed.
According to instructions from the Ministry of the Ministry of the Interior and the Ministry of Defence, the German special forces also participated indirectly in the rescue operation.
For example, specialists in the Federal Army as well as officials of GSG 9, the air squadrille of the Federal Police and the Federal Criminal Police Office, as well as logistics experts from Technischen Hilfwerk, were at the place of release.
As the spokeswoman of the Ministry communicated on Monday evening, the German forces were ultimately unrequited, the hostage takers having released their prisoners and fleeing when they acknowledged the imminence of release by force.
The Egyptian government reportedly accepted the aid offered by Germany to integrate it on the spot as a support force.
"Liberately it didn't happen until now," said a spokesperson for the Federal Ministry of the Interior.
The assagrunentists had kidnapped in southern Egypt the group of tourists composed of five Germans, five Italians, a Romanian and their eight Egyptian companions.
After being kidnapped in the desert in southern Egypt, the hostess were translated into the border area between Egypt, Libya, Chad and the Sudan.
Identity of miscarrions has so far remained vague, as have the details of release or possible ransom payments.
In the meantime, those who have been rescued have arrived safely in Cairo.
Steinmeier stated that the released hostages were under the protection of the German embassy.
He said nothing about the details of the release.
For the time being in Berlin, no information has been provided on the date of their return to Germany.
According to "Bild" magazine, the five vacationers and the special forces are expected to take off in an Air Force Airbus and arrive in Germany on Tuesday.
