Turkey is expanding its diplomatic horizons.
Long before Turkey planned to join the European Union, the European powers looked forward to entering its hinterland.
On the east side of the Bosporus, at 20 steps maybe, from Asia is one of the finest rebates of its fallen imperial ambition.
Hydarpasha Station was conceived in a Neo-Renaissance style by two German architects Otto Ritter and Helmut Conu.
At the same time, this is what the guides tell us.
The moniamus is arranged to straddle the platforms with observation tower at each of its corners, such as some German provincial town halls.
The stones are dirty.
The entire edifice is ruined.
But once we're inside the vault-wrapping ticket, we have no more doubt as to the grandeur of the company, which represents a transition to the west.
The Station was inaugurated in August 1908 by a German Anatolia-Baghddad Corporation.
Germany, lagged behind in imperialism, wanted to realize its medieval ambitions for domination thanks to this railroad arriving in Damascus, Baghdad as well as the land better known as Saudi Arabia.
German imperialist ambitions plummeted during World War I.
Its railways were attacked by Lawrence of Rabbis and its Arab irregulars.
The relative insecure of its surrounding area, energized by the icy Cold-Word borders, is over.
Britons and empire forces advanced in addition to Egypt and pushed the Turks and their German allies out of Palestine and Syria.
But there was also bad news for Hyderpasha Station.
In 1917, a train loaded with ammunition blew up destroying its subterranean roof.
The Turkish sources state that this is a sabotage.
Since then, it has slowly fallen into disrepair, engulfed in the twists of history.
Nowadays, the rail route to Anatolia and all the Oriental points serves at least symbolically to highlight the broadening of Turkey's diplomatic horizons.
The ruling Justice and Development Party, the AKP, has created a new foreign policy for the country.
The relative Isolation of its Neighplasity in Surrounded by the icy Cold-Criminal War
Now this is the time for a new policy of engagement.
The Turkish government has remarkable relations with Israel and Syria, the Palestinian factions of Hamas and Fatah, Iran and Iraq, and of course with the European Union and the United States.
In judging the parameters of this new foreign-policies, I embarked on a bed wagon at the Turkish capital, Ankara, to visit the Turkish MP AKP Suat Kiniklioglu, one of the foreign-policy experts.
The next morning, after a five-and-a-half-a-half journey, shackled on noisy, the pace of which retracted the old-fashioned old-fashioned world from the famous documentary The Night Mail, I finally arrived in the Turkish capital – presented as a new city with a new secular state, rapping its back to its German past.
But the AKP with its well-knapped Islamist roots represents an example of a change in the Turkish consensus.
He pretended the West, especially in Washington, D.C.
A cockroach accompanied me to AKP headquarters, an ultra-municipable structure, specifically built of stone and steel, in a developing neighborhood, encircled by half-builded shopping malls and many pastries in which the middle managers in waistcoat-watched men and women -- meet for morning business.
Suat Kiniklioglu could have been a center-right politician from any other party in Europe.
Sleeve, tie and he even has a Black Berry.
"the purpose of this foreign policy is now to re-enter Turkey in its immediate neighbourhood.
The Cold War was an anomaly", he said.
Turkey has been cut off from its rear country.
Now it has to look east and west at the same time.
This was not the case for both of them.
It had to do both.
'But at some some point, 'I've been wondering if 'Wouln't Turkey should it choose?".
"Turkish," he said "don't want to have a choice between the two."
He then pointed out an aspect that almost everyone with whom I was able to talk reciprocated to me, recited me by heart: the Turkish railway goes not only towards the Middle East, but also towards the Causacorsale, and around the Black Sea, it is therefore an advantage for Europe and the United States as for Turkey itself.
With the integration of Turkey, the message seems to be drawing a Europe travelling metaphorically on rail to Asia and the Middle East.
On my way back to Hydarpasha Station, after another non-sleeping, I redesigned these two Germans, Herr Ritter and Herr Conu, who followed the construction of this building 100 years ago.
They would indeed have been surprised to see that Europe’s ambitions in the Middle East could, for the most part, be in the hands of Turkey.
While I was waiting at the terminal, the ferry to cross the Bosphorus, I would like to remind myself of the period during which Turkey had been called 'the patient of Europe'.
At the very least, there is no longer any doubt on the continent that could claim this fascinating country.
Memories of Vernon Handley and William Woodruff.
Mark Brown writes: Music Director at Guildford since 1962, "Tod" Handley (deceased September 11) was responsible for the Guildford Philharmonic Orchestra and Choir.
Already part of his musical project for the city, he founded the following year the fantastic youth choir, Proteus.
The chorus was supposed to include those under 21, but before Proteus could sing its last song in 1979, many of us who were more than 20 years old - refused to abandon the chorus.
The name "Proteus" (which changed form according to mytology) represented the desire and skill of the choir to manage any type of music was presented to it, whether madrigal or Messianic.
Musical versatility produced by such a young chorus was often impressive, always exciting, thanks to the inspiration of a single man, and its enthusiasm for music, which remained constant over the years.
Remind that Tod repeated to us to "learn that the music speaks for itself".
He reportedly said, "The composer knows what he wants."
It's all in the music.
So sing."
Its baguette technique was absolutely clear, precise, even if never dictatorial nor appearing.
Regardless of the musical standard, he raised it much higher.
John Frayn Turner writes:
If a driver were to be named a knair, it was Vernon Handley.
I knew him well during the decades from 1962 to 1982, when he drove the Guildford Philharmonic.
Over the last 40 years, I've reviewed more than a hundred of his concerts, and I can honestly say that none of them has ever disappointed me -- and, a few times, they even inspired me.
Tod Handley possessed such a rare combination of grandeur and personal modesty.
He hated drivers who, according to him, "focus only on certain people."
Many of us will mislead.
William Woodruff
Prof. John Beckett wrote:
Your obituary of William Woodruff (deceased on 25 September) states that "in 1946, he had resumed his studies in economic history at Oxford and in 1950 became a member of Houblon-Norma research while he was doing a BSc, an MA and a PhD".
That's what came out of Woodruff Author in Beyond Nab End, but it's not correct.
From 1946 to 1953 he was Professor of Economic History at the University of Nottingham, and at the same time thrilled a PhD dissertation on the British Plastic Industry, under the supervision of Professor J.D. Chambers; it was later published in 1958 as The British Rubber Industry.
In the 1950s and 51s in the United States, he studied at Norman Hops, and in the summer of 1952 at the Universities of Illinois, Harvard and New York.
In 1953 he departed Nottingham for Illinois.
The reason why Woodruff chose to cover this part of his past remains unknown.
J. Vicente Resino, Faculty of Philosophy
