Second Assault for Honda Super Selling
In the month of November Honda will begin the commercialisation of the second-generation Jazz model.
If its predecessor already had a good habitability, this new single-volume has an even wider interior space.
Added to this there are some refined mechanics, which increase the driving comfort and make it possible to save more petrol.
An evolution in the face of a revolution.
So it's the second generation Honda Jazz.
Although aesthetically the new Honda Jazz presents a line very similar to the previous model, the changes introduced in this monovolume are plentiful.
These include an innovatory design, a new platform, larger dimensions and new engines.
However, before examining the new model, it must be stressed that this new Honda Jazz arrives six years after the first-generation market.
First of all, Honda renovated this model within a shorter time than that of other manufacturers.
Although Honda Jazz is less well known in Europe, this model is a phenomenon of sales in other markets.
This is the case of Japan, where the Honda Jazz has been the most marketed car in 2002 and also in 2008.
Precisely in Japan, nearly a million Honda Jazz have been sold since its launch.
The Old Continent, with more or less a million cars sold in six years, is in second place.
However, Honda claims that this new jazz will allow it to increase its sales in Europe.
Thus, while in 2007 the Japanese brand marketed 83,000 Honda Jazz, in 2009 it intends to sell 100,000, which would represent a 17% increase in sales on the European market.
On the Spanish market, Honda has marketed 8,000 Jazz since 2002, representing 6.7% of Honda sales in Spain.
The heads of the Spanish subsidiary are hoping to sell  2,000 Jazz in one year.
It is precisely the release of the new Jazz which will allow Honda to make much of the fall in sales in Spain.
Indeed, brand managers are hoping to end the year with 22,000 fewer cars, i.e. 4,000 fewer cars than in 2007, i.e. a fall in sales of 16%.
Thanks to this increase in sales, Honda increased its market share in Spain, reaching a market share of 1.8 per cent last year.
The Japanese brand also hopes to obtain a market share of 2 per cent in 2010.
Honda hopes to obtain this market share when the new model comes out.
This is the Insight, a five-door hybrid with a compact size - 4.37 meters in length - on which the Nippon company hopes to market nearly 200,000 cars per year around the world.
Depending on the markets and according to the Japanese manufacturer estimates, 50 per cent of Insight sales in the United States, while the remaining other half will be divided between the Old Container and Japan.
The Insight commercial launch is expected in the first three months of next year, following its presentation to the public in January in Valence.
Compared to the first generation, the new Jazz increases its dimensions.
Its length reaches 3.90 meters (five centimetres more), its width 1.69 meters (an additional two centimetres) and the distance between its shafts is 2.50 meters, which represents an increase of five centimetres.
On the other hand, the height does not change either 1.52 metres.
The dimensions are also increasing and the inner space increases.
On the one hand, this gain is due, on the one hand, to the introduction of a gasoline reserve on the other, a solution also adopted for the Honda Jazz.
In the same way, the trunk capacity – which has a double-bottom system – can contain up to 399 liters, an additional 19-strength trunk compared to the trunk of the first generation.
It should be pointed out that these dimensions are those of the version with a 1.4L engine, since the trunk capacity of the Honda Jazz 1.2L is 379 litres.
The other innovation - in addition to exterior elements such as hanging lights or scenic roofs - is the improvement of access to rear seats.
Doors which give access to the rear seating have an aperture angle of 80 degrees from the rear doors of the first jazz which opened with an angle of 67 degrees.
The engines are also new to this second generation Jazz.
At the beginning of its commercialisation, this small model will be equipped with two 1.2 and 1.4-litre gasoline propellers.
Both engines have an i-VTEC technology that reduces gasoline consumption and CO2 emissions, which makes the engine more powerful and increases engine speed.
The first 1.2-litre engine developed 90 horsepower at 6,000 rpm 6,000 rpm and torque at 4,900 rpm.
This propellant - which in the previous jazz reached 78 horsepower - has a petrol consumption of 5.3 litres per 100 km and emissions of 125 grams of CO2.
Compared to the previous propellant, which in the previous generation reached 83 horsepower, now reaches 100 horses.
This Honda Jazz 1.4L of 100 horses has a combined consumption of 5.4 liters per 100 km and emissions of 128 gram of CO2.
'Car bee' which avoids collisions
In order to advance the development of technologies for the prevention of accidents, Nissan drew, this time, from nature, in particular bees.
The Japanese company has created a robot which, imitating the flight of bees, avoids accidents.
It is called Bio-Mimetic Car Robot Drive or BR23C and represents the first steps towards Collision Prevention Technologies.
Engineers at the Advanced Technical Center of Nissan Motor Co. built this small robot in collaboration with the Center for Advanced Science and Technology Research at the prestigious University of Tokyo, Japan.
According to Kazuhiro Doi, Managing Director of Public Relations for Technology and Research and Brand Management Development, the BR23C represents the development of the Nissan Security Shield.
This robot will allow branded vehicles to prevent collisions.
During its flight, each bee creates its own personal space in an oval shape that is very similar to the Nissan Security Shield.
But the most important of these are the bee-composed eyes, capable of seeing in an angle of more than 300 degrees and in all directions, which allows it to fly without interruption within its own personal space.
To recreate this visual function, the engineers developed so-called Laser Range Finder.
The latter detects the obstacles which lie over a distance of two meters and within a radius of 180 degrees, calculates the distance to this object and sends a signal to the microprocessor, immediately, in order to avoid the obstacle.
'At the moment it detects an obstacle, the small robot will militate the movements of a bee and will immediately change direction by bifurcating its wheels of 90 degrees or more to avoid the collision.
The greatest difference compared to any other existing system is that the escaping maneuver is totally instinctive.
If that were the case, the robot wouldn't be able to react quickly enough to avoid the obstacles, 'explain Toshiyuki Andoh, Director of the Displacement Laboratory and chief engineer of this project.
Contrary to a bee, the robot cannot be diverted upwards or downwards or diagonally devoid; it can move only in two dimensions and depending on what its wheels allow it and not in infinite directions such as bees.
For this reason, Nissan used a function of rotation, but also of acceleration and deceleration, so that the small robot could avoid obstacles.
' Every second this device must only process information and act according to this data.
It doesn't have to keep the information or the intuitive logic of prior manoeuvength, which is why it can operate continuously with only a few dozen memory KB.
It may come as a surprise, but we're not talking about gigabytes of memory or even megabytes."
This represents the first development of new instantaneous Collision prevention technologies, a technology that will be introduced into production cars in about ten years according to Nissan.
Robot works with lithium-polymer batteries, which are lighter and last longer than lithium-ion batteries.
The other possible possible application of this technology in the future could be the adaptation of this device on a GPS satellite navigation system to guide the driver to its destination while avoiding obstacles on the road.
